course: dictionary
lesson: 9443
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them show which meaning you need. This is the context. The word stalk is a good example. In «Rhubarb stalks are safe to eat cooked», rhubarb is a plant, so the stalk must be a plant part: the thin stem.
explain: «Jumping spiders build no catching web: they stalk and leap.» Here stalk is a doing word. A spider cannot be a plant stem, so the clue is that it is hunting. Spiders creep up quietly on their prey, then leap. Try your meaning in the sentence: "they creep up quietly and leap" makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Stalk can also mean walking in a stiff, angry way. «She stalked out of the room» tells you she was cross and proud, not that she was hunting anything. Read the whole sentence, and ask: what would fit here?
fact: As a noun, a stalk is the thin main stem of a plant, or the small stem that holds a leaf, flower or fruit.
fact: The stalk carries water up from the roots to the leaves and holds the plant upright.
fact: To stalk an animal means to follow it slowly and quietly to catch it, as a cat or a lion does.
fact: To stalk off or stalk out means to walk away in a stiff, angry, proud way.
fact: To stalk a person means to follow or watch them again and again in a frightening way. This is wrong and against the law, so tell a trusted adult.
exercise: «Rhubarb stalks are safe to eat cooked, but rhubarb leaves are poisonous.» In this sentence, what is a stalk? | A sharp tool for cutting wood | A loud noise in the night | *The thin stem of a plant | A small animal that hides in the grass
exercise: Jumping spiders «stalk and leap» to catch their prey. What does stalk mean here? | To walk away angrily | *To creep up quietly after an animal to catch it | To fall asleep in the sun | To spin a big web and wait for a fly
exercise: Copy the word from this sentence that means a plant part: «After a peanut flower fades its stalk pushes into the soil.» | fades | *stalk | soil | pushes
test: «She stalked out of the room, angry and proud.» What does stalked out mean? | Hid under the table | Ran out laughing | *Walked away stiffly in anger | Followed the room quietly
test: Someone keeps following you and messaging you after you have asked them to stop. What should you do? | *Tell a trusted adult straight away | Keep it secret and hope it stops | Follow them back to see why | Send them a rude reply
exam: Which statement about the word stalk is true? | Stalk is always a bad word | Stalk only means a plant stem | It only means to climb a tree | *It can be a noun for a plant stem, and a verb meaning to follow quietly or to walk away stiffly
